// campaigns domain -- email marketing campaigns as ordinary graph state
// (memql#3323).
//
// The whole point of modelling a campaign as CONCEPTS rather than as a
// bespoke table plus a bespoke API is that everything downstream then comes
// for free: the rows are browsable in the portal's concept browser, they are
// composable into views through the shared element library, they carry
// history like every other row, and they are authorization-gated by the same
// per-row machinery as the rest of the graph. A campaign editor is then a
// layout over queries and mutations, not a new subsystem.
//
// SEVEN CONCEPTS. Five answer the four questions a send has to answer --
// who, what, when, and what happened -- and two are the sending engine's
// own state (memql#3348):
//
//   audience     WHO        a named set of addresses
//   recipient    WHO        one address in an audience, with its subscription state
//   template     WHAT       the subject + bodies, authored once and reused
//   campaign     WHEN       audience x template x schedule, plus the send state
//   delivery     OUTCOME    one row per recipient per campaign
//   sendJob      ENGINE     the per-campaign send run the worker drains
//   suppression  ENGINE     the cluster-wide do-not-mail list, digest-keyed
//
// PER-ROW AUTHZ TIER: owned, on the five operator-facing concepts. Every row
// carries ownerUserId, every concept declares @rowAuthz(owner="ownerUserId"),
// every mutation stamps that field from actor.userId rather than from a caller
// argument, and every query names it as a top-level conjunct. A caller
// therefore cannot read, create, retarget or delete another operator's
// campaign even with its id. Shared / team-owned campaigns are a deliberate
// non-goal for now: "owned" is the tier that is enforced end to end today, and
// widening it later is a tier change plus a grant concept, not a re-model.
//
// THE TWO ENGINE CONCEPTS ARE clusterOwner, NOT owned, and the split is the
// load-bearing part of the sending design (memql#3348).
//
//   `sendJob` is the queue the worker scans. A worker cannot scan an OWNED
//   concept at all -- the owned tier injects `ownerUserId==actor.userId` into
//   every read and has no cluster-owner bypass (rowauthz_enforce.go), so
//   "find every campaign that wants sending" is not a question an owned row
//   can answer. It is also not a question that SHOULD be answerable by a
//   caller-writable row: whichever row says "send campaign X as user Y" is,
//   by construction, an instruction the engine will carry out under Y's
//   authority. So the job row is clusterOwner-tier and every write to it is
//   internal-origin Go that derived campaignOwnerUserId from a row it had
//   already read UNDER THE CALLER'S OWN ACTOR. The caller proves ownership by
//   being able to read their own campaign; nothing they send is trusted.
//
//   `suppression` is the do-not-mail list, and it OUTRANKS EVERY AUDIENCE.
//   That phrase only means something if the list is one list: an unsubscribe
//   is a statement by a person to the operator of this deployment, and every
//   campaign leaves through the same authenticated mailbox and the same SPF /
//   DKIM records. A per-operator list would let operator B mail an address
//   that unsubscribed from operator A under a From: header the recipient
//   cannot tell apart -- one domain, one reputation, one complaint. So the
//   list is cluster-wide, and it is keyed by a SHA-256 digest of the
//   normalized address rather than by the address itself, so being
//   cluster-wide costs nobody an address: a caller can ask "is this address
//   suppressed" only about an address it already holds, and reading the whole
//   table yields digests and domains, never a mailbox.
//
// WHAT THE SENDING ENGINE IS: component/campaigns (the drain worker) plus
// integrations/campaigns (the DSL-callable surface). It sends DIRECTLY through
// the email integration rather than staging v1:platform:outboundRequest rows,
// which is a change from what this header said before memql#3348 -- see the
// `delivery.outboundRequestId` field doc for the three reasons.

use identity.concepts.{ user }
use platform.concepts.{ outboundRequest }

/// A named set of email recipients -- the WHO of a campaign. Audiences are authored in the portal
/// and referenced by campaigns; membership lives on v1:campaigns:recipient rows pointing back here
/// rather than as a list on this row, so a hundred-thousand-address audience is a hundred thousand
/// rows rather than one enormous payload. Per-row authz: owned -- ownerUserId is stamped from
/// actor.userId on every write and every read gates on ownerUserId==actor.userId.
@displayCard(primary="name", secondary="description", tertiary="ownerUserId", status="status")
@rowAuthz(owner="ownerUserId")
concept audience {
  ownerUserId  string!  @description("v1:identity:user.id that owns this audience. Stamped from actor.userId at create time and re-stamped on every update, so ownership can never be reassigned by a caller. The load-bearing per-row authz key.")
  name         string!  @description("Short operator-facing name for the audience, e.g. 'Beta waitlist'. The primary display field; not required to be unique.")
  description  string   @description("What this audience is and how someone lands in it. Free text, shown under the name in the portal's audience list.")
  status       enum("active", "archived")  @default("active")  @description("Lifecycle. 'active' audiences are offered when authoring a campaign; 'archived' ones are kept for the history their past campaigns reference but are hidden from the picker. Audiences are never deleted -- a deleted audience would orphan every delivery record that names it.")

  @relationship(type="parent", field="ownerUserId", target=user, direction="outgoing")
}

/// One email address in an audience, with the subscription state that decides whether it may be
/// mailed. Subscription state is modelled here rather than derived at send time because it is a
/// standing fact about a person, not a property of one campaign: an unsubscribe must suppress every
/// future send, including sends of campaigns authored before the unsubscribe. Per-row authz: owned
/// via ownerUserId, mirrored from the parent audience at create time.
@displayCard(primary="email", secondary="displayName", tertiary="audienceId", status="subscriptionStatus")
@rowAuthz(owner="ownerUserId")
concept recipient {
  ownerUserId         string!  @description("v1:identity:user.id that owns this recipient row -- the owner of the parent audience, stamped from actor.userId. The per-row authz key.")
  audienceId          string!  @description("v1:campaigns:audience.id this address belongs to. A person in two audiences is two rows, so a per-audience unsubscribe is expressible and a global one is a sweep.")
  email               string!  @pii  @description("The recipient's email address -- the delivery target and the identity of the row. Marked @pii: it is scrubbed by the deletion path and every query projecting it must constrain its caller.")
  displayName         string   @pii  @description("Optional human name used to personalize the greeting. Marked @pii for the same reason as email.")
  subscriptionStatus  enum("subscribed", "unsubscribed", "bounced", "complained")  @default("subscribed")  @description("Whether this address may be mailed. 'subscribed' is the only sendable state. 'unsubscribed' is the recipient's own choice and is permanent until they opt back in; 'bounced' and 'complained' are the provider's verdict and exist as distinct states because they mean different things to a deliverability review -- a bounce is a bad address, a complaint is a reputational event.")
  unsubscribedAt      datetime  @description("When the address left the subscribed state. Empty while subscribed. Recorded rather than inferred from row history so a suppression check is one field read.")
  source              enum("manual", "import", "signup")  @default("manual")  @description("How the address got here. 'manual' = typed into the portal, 'import' = bulk upload, 'signup' = a product flow added it. Provenance a deliverability review asks for first when complaint rates rise.")

  @relationship(type="parent", field="ownerUserId", target=user, direction="outgoing")
  @relationship(type="parent", field="audienceId", target=audience, direction="outgoing")
}

/// The authored content of a campaign -- subject line plus plain-text and optional HTML bodies. Kept
/// separate from the campaign so one piece of content can be sent to several audiences without being
/// retyped, and so editing copy is a different action (and a different authorization decision, later)
/// from scheduling a send. Per-row authz: owned.
@displayCard(primary="name", secondary="subject", tertiary="ownerUserId", status="status")
@rowAuthz(owner="ownerUserId")
concept template {
  ownerUserId  string!  @description("v1:identity:user.id that owns this template. Stamped from actor.userId. The per-row authz key.")
  name         string!  @description("Operator-facing name for the template, e.g. 'August product update'. Distinct from the subject line, which is what the recipient sees.")
  subject      string!  @description("The email subject line. Rendered verbatim; the engine's email sender rejects control characters in it (header-injection barrier in integrations/email).")
  textBody     string!  @description("The plain-text body. Required, not optional: a text alternative is what keeps a message out of the spam bucket and readable in a client that refuses HTML.")
  htmlBody     string   @description("Optional HTML alternative. When present the message goes out as multipart/alternative with text first.")
  status       enum("draft", "ready", "archived")  @default("draft")  @description("Authoring lifecycle. 'draft' is being written; 'ready' means an operator has decided it may be attached to a campaign; 'archived' keeps it readable for the campaigns that referenced it without offering it in the picker.")

  @relationship(type="parent", field="ownerUserId", target=user, direction="outgoing")
}

/// A campaign: one template addressed to one audience, optionally at a scheduled time, plus the state
/// of its send. This is the row an operator thinks of as 'the campaign'. Per-row authz: owned.
///
/// The send-state fields (startedAt / completedAt / the three counters / lastError) are written by
/// the sending engine's drain worker through `updateCampaignProgress`, which runs under the
/// CAMPAIGN OWNER'S actor -- so `ownerUserId` is re-stamped from `actor.userId` there exactly as it
/// is on the operator-facing writes, and the counters land on a row the worker was authorized to
/// touch rather than on one it reached past the owner tier.
@displayCard(primary="name", secondary="scheduledAt", tertiary="audienceId", status="status")
@rowAuthz(owner="ownerUserId")
concept campaign {
  ownerUserId     string!  @description("v1:identity:user.id that owns this campaign. Stamped from actor.userId. The per-row authz key.")
  name            string!  @description("Operator-facing campaign name. The primary display field.")
  audienceId      string!  @description("v1:campaigns:audience.id this campaign is addressed to. Resolved to recipient rows at send time, not at authoring time, so an audience that grows between scheduling and sending is mailed as it stands when the send runs.")
  templateId      string!  @description("v1:campaigns:template.id supplying the subject and bodies.")
  fromName        string   @description("Per-campaign display name on the From header, e.g. 'The memQL team'. A non-secret presentation setting: it overrides the integration-wide default (MEMQL_EMAIL_FROM_NAME) for this campaign only. The From ADDRESS is deliberately not settable here -- it is bound to the configured mailbox credential and changing it is a deliverability decision, not a campaign one.")
  replyTo         string   @description("Optional Reply-To address for this campaign. Non-secret; empty means replies go to the configured sender address.")
  scheduledAt     datetime  @description("When the send should begin. Empty means the campaign is not scheduled -- it is a draft an operator will start by hand.")
  status          enum("draft", "scheduled", "sending", "paused", "sent", "cancelled", "failed")  @default("draft")  @description("Campaign lifecycle. 'draft' is being authored; 'scheduled' is committed to a time; 'sending' / 'paused' / 'sent' / 'failed' are send-engine states; 'cancelled' is an operator stopping it before or during a send. Only 'draft' and 'scheduled' are reachable today -- the transitions past them belong to the sending engine.")
  startedAt       datetime  @description("When the send actually began. Empty until it does. Distinct from scheduledAt: the gap between the two is the queue latency a deliverability review looks at.")
  completedAt     datetime  @description("When the last recipient reached a terminal delivery state.")
  recipientCount  int  @default("0")  @description("How many recipients the audience resolved to when the send started. Frozen at send time so the counters below add up even if the audience changes afterwards.")
  sentCount       int  @default("0")  @description("Deliveries the transport accepted. Denormalized from v1:campaigns:delivery so a campaign list does not have to aggregate per row.")
  failedCount     int  @default("0")  @description("Deliveries that reached a terminal failure.")
  lastError       string   @description("The most recent send-level error -- a refused configuration, an exhausted allowlist -- as opposed to a per-recipient failure, which lives on the delivery row. Empty on a clean send.")

  @relationship(type="parent", field="ownerUserId", target=user, direction="outgoing")
  @relationship(type="interactsWith", field="audienceId", target=audience, direction="outgoing")
  @relationship(type="interactsWith", field="templateId", target=template, direction="outgoing")
}

/// One recipient's outcome for one campaign -- the per-recipient audit trail a deliverability review
/// is actually made of, and the LEDGER THAT MAKES A RESUMED SEND SAFE. Per-row authz: owned.
///
/// IDEMPOTENCY IS STRUCTURAL, not a retry flag. The row id is derived deterministically from
/// (campaignId, recipientId) -- per-part hashed, so no separator can alias two pairs onto one id --
/// which means a recipient has exactly one delivery row per campaign no matter how many times the
/// send is resumed, restarted or raced between replicas. The worker's batch is "recipients of this
/// audience with no delivery row, plus rows still `pending` whose nextAttemptAt has passed", so a
/// recipient that already reached `sent` or `skipped` is simply not in the next batch. There is no
/// "already sent?" flag to forget to check: the absence of the row IS the work queue.
///
/// Written by component/campaigns' drain worker through `recordCampaignDelivery`, which runs under
/// the CAMPAIGN OWNER'S actor -- so `ownerUserId` is stamped from `actor.userId` like every other
/// write in this domain, never from a caller argument. That is what memql#3348 supplies and what
/// took v1:campaigns:delivery off the owner-stamping gate's exemption list.
///
/// outboundRequestId is EMPTY on every row this engine writes, and that is a decision rather than an
/// omission (memql#3348). memql#3323 designed the send to stage v1:platform:outboundRequest rows and
/// let the engine's outbound worker deliver them. Three things make that the wrong seam for bulk
/// campaign mail: (1) the campaign worker has to SEE the provider's 429 + Retry-After to pace
/// itself, and a queue drained by another worker turns that signal into an opaque retry -- which is
/// exactly the "retries that look like duplicates to the recipient" failure; (2) a per-recipient
/// TERMINAL outcome is the whole point of this row, and an async seam leaves every row `pending`
/// until somebody writes a reconciler; (3) MEMQL_OUTBOUND_EMAIL_ALLOWLIST is a domain allowlist for
/// TRANSACTIONAL mail, and a marketing audience is by definition arbitrary external domains -- so
/// routing campaigns through it would either refuse every send or force that allowlist wide open and
/// weaken the transactional egress control it exists to provide. The field stays on the schema
/// because a future queued transport would populate it.
@displayCard(primary="email", secondary="campaignId", tertiary="sentAt", status="status")
@rowAuthz(owner="ownerUserId")
concept delivery {
  ownerUserId        string!  @description("v1:identity:user.id that owns this delivery record -- the owner of the campaign, mirrored so the row is gated without a join. The per-row authz key.")
  campaignId         string!  @description("v1:campaigns:campaign.id this delivery belongs to.")
  recipientId        string!  @description("v1:campaigns:recipient.id that was targeted.")
  email              string!  @pii  @description("The address the send targeted, captured at send time rather than read back from the recipient row -- an address that changes afterwards must not rewrite what was actually mailed. Marked @pii.")
  status             enum("pending", "sent", "failed", "skipped")!  @description("Outcome for this recipient. 'sent' and 'skipped' and 'failed' are terminal; 'pending' means a retryable transport failure whose next attempt is due at nextAttemptAt (a row is never written as 'pending' before an attempt -- the ABSENCE of the row is the not-yet-attempted state, see the concept doc). 'skipped' is the suppression case -- unsubscribed, hard-bounced or complained -- and is distinct from 'failed' because it is a correct outcome rather than an error.")
  outboundRequestId  string   @description("v1:platform:outboundRequest.id carrying this message, when a queued transport staged one. EMPTY on every row the current engine writes -- it delivers directly so it can observe the provider's throttle response. See the concept doc for the three reasons.")
  skipReason         string   @description("Why a 'skipped' delivery was suppressed: 'unsubscribed' / 'hard_bounce' / 'complaint' / 'manual' when the cluster suppression list matched, or the recipient row's own subscriptionStatus when that was the gate. Empty otherwise.")
  lastError          string   @description("The transport's error for a 'failed' or retrying 'pending' delivery, truncated to 4KB. Empty otherwise.")
  sentAt             datetime  @description("When the transport accepted the message. Empty unless status is 'sent'.")
  attempts           int  @default("0")  @description("Delivery attempts made for this recipient. Incremented on each transport failure and bounded by MEMQL_CAMPAIGNS_MAX_ATTEMPTS, past which the row goes terminal 'failed'. Zero on a 'skipped' row, which never reaches the transport.")
  nextAttemptAt      datetime  @description("When a retryable 'pending' row becomes eligible again -- bounded exponential backoff with jitter, or the provider's own Retry-After when it supplied one. Compared Go-side by the drain worker, which is why it is a stored instant rather than a computed one.")

  @relationship(type="parent", field="ownerUserId", target=user, direction="outgoing")
  @relationship(type="parent", field="campaignId", target=campaign, direction="outgoing")
  @relationship(type="interactsWith", field="recipientId", target=recipient, direction="outgoing")
  @relationship(type="interactsWith", field="outboundRequestId", target=outboundRequest, direction="outgoing")
}

/// The engine's own record of one send RUN -- the queue the drain worker scans, and the only row in
/// this domain the worker can find without already knowing whose it is.
///
/// WHY THIS IS clusterOwner AND NOT owned. A worker cannot scan an owned concept: the owned tier
/// injects `ownerUserId==actor.userId` into every read and there is no cluster-owner bypass on it
/// (component/memql/rowauthz_enforce.go), so "which campaigns want sending" is not a question an
/// owned row can answer. Nor should it be answerable from a row a caller can write: whichever row
/// says "send campaign X as user Y" is an instruction the engine carries out under Y's authority, so
/// a caller-writable one is an escalation with extra steps. Both writers of this concept
/// (campaignStartSend and the worker) are Go that derived campaignOwnerUserId from a campaign row it
/// had ALREADY READ under the caller's own actor -- the caller proves ownership by being able to
/// read their own campaign, and nothing they typed is trusted.
///
/// One row per campaign, id = the campaign's bare short id, so pause / resume / restart all land on
/// the same timeline rather than accumulating runs. Per-recipient idempotency does not live here --
/// it lives on the delivery ledger (see v1:campaigns:delivery), which is what makes a resumed send
/// safe even if this row is lost entirely.
@displayCard(primary="campaignId", secondary="status", tertiary="startedAt", status="status")
@rowAuthz(clusterOwner)
concept sendJob {
  campaignId           string!  @description("v1:campaigns:campaign.id this job drains. Stored canonical like every other @relationship field; the worker bare-ifies it (memql.BareShortId) before feeding it back to campaignById, because a named-query argument is the BARE-ids client contract.")
  campaignOwnerUserId  string!  @description("v1:identity:user.id the worker impersonates for every owned-tier read and write this job performs -- the campaign, its template, its audience's recipients, and the delivery ledger. SERVER-DERIVED ONLY: both writers read it off a campaign row they had already read under the caller's own actor, so it can never name a user the caller could not already act as. This is the single most security-load-bearing field in the domain.")
  audienceId           string!  @description("v1:campaigns:audience.id resolved to recipients each batch, so an audience that grows mid-send is mailed as it stands when the batch runs.")
  templateId           string!  @description("v1:campaigns:template.id supplying the subject and bodies.")
  status               enum("queued", "running", "paused", "completed", "failed", "cancelled")!  @description("Job lifecycle. 'queued' is enqueued and unclaimed; 'running' has had at least one batch; 'paused' is an operator stopping the drain without discarding the ledger; 'completed' means every recipient reached a terminal delivery state; 'failed' is a job-level refusal (no configured sender, no unsubscribe secret, audience over the ceiling); 'cancelled' follows the campaign being cancelled.")
  recipientCount       int  @default("0")  @description("Recipients the audience resolved to on the most recent batch. Not frozen -- the campaign row carries the frozen count.")
  sentCount            int  @default("0")  @description("Deliveries the transport accepted so far.")
  skippedCount         int  @default("0")  @description("Recipients suppressed rather than mailed. The gap between this and sentCount IS the suppression rate a deliverability review asks for.")
  failedCount          int  @default("0")  @description("Recipients that reached a terminal failure.")
  lastError            string  @description("The most recent job-level error, truncated. Empty on a clean run.")
  startedAt            datetime  @description("When the first batch ran.")
  completedAt          datetime  @description("When the job reached a terminal status.")
  throttledUntil       datetime  @description("When the provider's own throttle response says we may resume. Set from a 429/503 Retry-After; the worker leaves the job alone until it passes. Distinct from our own pacing, which is a per-process token bucket and needs no row.")

  @relationship(type="parent", field="campaignId", target=campaign, direction="outgoing")
  @relationship(type="interactsWith", field="campaignOwnerUserId", target=user, direction="outgoing")
  @relationship(type="interactsWith", field="audienceId", target=audience, direction="outgoing")
  @relationship(type="interactsWith", field="templateId", target=template, direction="outgoing")
}

/// The cluster-wide do-not-mail list. ONE address per row, keyed by the SHA-256 hex digest of the
/// normalized address (lowercased, trimmed) -- the digest IS the row id, so a lookup is a by-id read
/// and a re-suppression is a new version of the same row rather than a duplicate.
///
/// SUPPRESSION IS CLUSTER-WIDE, NOT PER-ACCOUNT, and that is the decision this concept exists to
/// record (memql#3348). An unsubscribe is a statement by a person to the operator of THIS
/// DEPLOYMENT: every campaign leaves through the same authenticated mailbox, under the same From
/// address, signed by the same SPF and DKIM records. A per-operator list would let operator B mail
/// an address that unsubscribed from operator A, in a message the recipient cannot tell apart from
/// the one they already opted out of -- and the complaint lands on the one shared domain reputation.
/// Legally the obligation attaches to the sender, and there is one sender.
///
/// BEING CLUSTER-WIDE COSTS NOBODY AN ADDRESS. The row stores no mailbox: the id is a non-reversible
/// digest and the only human-readable field is the address's DOMAIN, which is what a deliverability
/// review actually asks for ("which domains are bouncing?") and which identifies no one. A caller
/// can therefore ask "is this address suppressed" only about an address it already holds.
///
/// Reads and writes are clusterOwner because the list must not be caller-writable: a caller who
/// could insert digests could silently suppress a competitor's audience, and one who could delete
/// them could un-suppress a complaint. Every write goes through the engine -- the unsubscribe
/// endpoint, the feedback builtin, or an operator's explicit suppress call -- and each of those
/// derives the address from something it verified rather than from an argument. An operator's own
/// view of who left is their recipient rows' subscriptionStatus, which the worker converges onto
/// this list on the next send.
@displayCard(primary="domain", secondary="reason", tertiary="suppressedAt", status="reason")
@rowAuthz(clusterOwner)
concept suppression {
  reason            enum("unsubscribed", "hard_bounce", "complaint", "manual")!  @description("Why this address may never be mailed again. 'unsubscribed' is the recipient's own choice, via the RFC 8058 one-click header or the link in the body. 'hard_bounce' and 'complaint' are the provider's verdict and are kept apart because they mean different things to a review -- a bounce is a dead address, a complaint is a reputational event. 'manual' is an operator adding one by hand.")
  domain            string  @description("The domain part of the suppressed address, lowercased. Recorded rather than derived because the address itself is never stored: it is what makes 'which domains are bouncing' answerable without making 'who unsubscribed' answerable.")
  suppressedAt      datetime!  @description("When the address entered the list. Stamped from the engine clock at write time, not threaded by a caller -- unlike a recipient row's unsubscribedAt, which can legitimately record a state change that happened earlier.")
  sourceCampaignId  string  @description("v1:campaigns:campaign.id of the send that produced this suppression, when one did. Empty for an operator-entered row or a feedback report that named no campaign.")
  note              string  @description("Free-text provenance for a 'manual' row, or the provider's bounce classification for a reported one. Never the address.")

  @relationship(type="interactsWith", field="sourceCampaignId", target=campaign, direction="outgoing")
}
